 ## Overview
-Random Offset Annealed Fourier Encoding (ROAFE) is a positional encoding scheme for 3D coordinates that combines Fourier basis functions with random Gaussian offsets and an annealing schedule to improve the optimization landscape for neural radiance fields. It addresses the axis-alignment artifacts and striping issues inherent in standard NeRF encoding by breaking symmetry through random shifts, while using a truncated Hann window to gradually introduce high-frequency details. It is primarily used in the SAMURAI framework.
+Random Offset Annealed Fourier Encoding (ROAFE) is a positional encoding scheme for radiance fields that modifies standard Fourier encoding by introducing random Gaussian offsets to frequency basis functions and applying an annealing schedule to the frequencies [S1]. It solves the problem of axis-aligned frequency artifacts (striping) inherent in standard NeRF encoding while maintaining the optimization stability provided by frequency annealing [S1]. It is distinguished from standard NeRF encoding by the addition of random offsets and from BARF/Nerfies by the specific handling of these offsets within the annealing window [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-- Input: A 3D coordinate vector $x \in \mathbb{R}^3$.
-- Output: An encoded vector $\gamma(x) \in \mathbb{R}^{3+6L}$, where $L$ is the number of frequency bands.
